Since eigenvalues are scalar quantities, their order can often tell us something about the structure of the matrix. For a symmetric real matrix, the two eigenvalues will be real and the larger of the two will be positive and large. If the matrix is defined only by its eigenvectors, this tells us something about the "size" or "stretching" of its vectors. Positive eigenvalues mean stretching in one direction and shrinking in another. And the larger the eigenvalue, the greater the stretching. If two eigenvalues are complex-conjugate, that indicates a rotation in the vector space. If all eigenvalues are real and all are negative, the matrix represents a shrinking only—it's flipping the sign of every element.  Exercise 1:Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the following matrix: `[[4, -2], [1, 3]]`
