A non-unique EigenvalueWhen we try to solve a system of linear equations like so: Ax = λx, we find that λ is an eigenvalue for A, and x is the corresponding eigenvector.  However, we notice that we get infinitely many solution vectors (since the solution to our equation is essentially all vectors proportional to x, and any vector proportional to x is a solution).This is due to the fact that the domain of the matrix-vector multiplication operation includes all possible vectors, which is why we end up with every vector whose direction corresponds to the eigenvector.This leads us to the definition of the eigenvectors as the solution vectors that satisfy the equation (A- λI)v = 0, but in this case we’ve used the fact that the determinant of [A - λI] is zero for the solutions to be linearly independent (i.e., their dot product is nonzero), which gives us all possible solutions. As such, we end up with infinitely many possible eigenvectors that satisfy (A- λI)v = 0. This is why we say eigenvalues need to be unique, i.e., every matrix has one and only one eigenvector with the corresponding eigenvalue; in the case where multiple eigenvectors exist, they are linearly dependent, and there are infinitely many possible solutions to (A- λI)v = 0. The statement that multiple eigenvectors correspond to one eigenvalue refers to the fact that the matrix acts identically on these linearly dependent eigenvectors.
